  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nCooler and a much less humid airmass will move in behind the\ncold front Tuesday night into Wednesday. Afternoon high \ntemperatures will be closer to 80 degrees across central \nMissouri and the eastern Ozarks but closer to 90 degrees across \nextreme southwest Missouri and southeast Kansas; regardless, \nmuch lower dew points around 60 degrees will make it feel much \nmore comfortable than the heat of late. These relatively cooler \nconditions will persist through Friday, followed by a warming \ntrend through the weekend. After previously large NBM \ntemperature spreads on Friday, guidance has trended toward a \ncooler solution with increasing confidence in showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nTo that end, global models begin to flatten the ridge as \nanother shortwave trough moves through the area late Thursday \nwhich brings the next chances for rain into Friday morning.\nRain chances have increased across the board, now between 40 to\n70%, with the highest chances across central Missouri. \n\nFor the weekend and beyond, ensemble clusters have exhibited\nslightly better agreement in the evolution of the upper-level\npattern with general consensus in maintaining the ridge across \nthe western/central CONUS, but important differences still \nremain. With the ridge axis to the west, there typically is not\na screaming, widespread precipitation signal in the guidance\nthis far out, but it does leave the door open to the passage of\nshortwave impulses on the eastern periphery of the ridge that\ncould result in rain chances here in southwest Missouri and\nsoutheast Kansas. Any potential would have to be better \nassessed in future forecast packages. Otherwise, PoPs remain \nless than 20% areawide Saturday afternoon through Tuesday.",
